call's recorded assignment in the order \"'`Some((idx, _)) if idx <= decided` first and `Some((idx, ballot)) if ballot < cur_ballot` second (actor.rs:201-222), so index coverage alone resolves the call \"\n   \"Ok(idx) without establishing that the entry at idx is the one the call submitted; the Superseded arm exists for the leadership-change case and its own documentation concedes the entry may never be decided (event.rs:45-60), while the Ok contract is to await the entry'\"'s decided log index \\\"\n   \\\"(handle.rs:114-137). Retained execution 78c53df3891643edafaa4f10d60ea5d1 (experiment 1660067f6fc64af7a72dcbb7428e8a53-experiment, exit 0, both cases passed, parsing_error_count 0) carries both sides of the boundary in one run. Event 1 \"'`overwritten_index_resolution` \"\n   \"{a_decided_idx: 1, a_entry_at_index_1_is_own: false, b_decided_idx: 1, b_idx: 1, resolution: '\"\\\\\\\"Ok(1)\\\\\\\"}: node 1's entry was accepted only locally (node 1 isolated in both directions), node 2 took over with an empty log and decided index 1 with its own entry, and after node 1 re-synchronized its \\\"\n   \\\"call resolved Ok(1) although the entry at index 1 was node 2's - a false completion, with the Superseded heuristic unable to fire because the index-based arm is matched first. Event 2 \"'`adopted_entry_resolution` {a_decided_before: 0, a_decided_idx: 1, a_entry_at_index_1_is_own: true, \"\n   \"b_decided_idx: 1, resolution: '\"\\\\\\\"Ok(1)\\\\\\\"}: with both followers accepting node 1's entry (their receipts dropped, so node 1 stayed at decided 0 with its call pending), node 2 adopted the followers' log containing node 1's entry and decided index 1; after node 1 re-synchronized, the same call \\\"\n   \\\"resolved Ok(1) and the entry at index 1 was node 1's own - the truthful case. Event 0 \"'`after_b_takeover` {b_leader: '\"\\\\\\\"2:true\\\\\\\", c_leader: \\\\\\\"2:true\\\\\\\", a_leader: \\\\\\\"1:true\\\\\\\", b_decided: 0, c_decided: 0} records the state in which the unadopted case's substitution happens. The comparison therefore holds and its scope is bounded by \\\"\n   \\\"measurement, not argument: the false completion needs the caller's entry not to have been adopted by the following leader (no quorum acceptance, or acceptance hidden from that leader), while an entry that was adopted is reported truthfully - consistent with the adoption path (prepare-phase Promise log_sync and \\\"\n   \\\"take_max_promise_sync, leader.rs:257-285 / follower.rs:13-51) which keeps quorum-accepted values at their index. Counter-limitations kept in force: (a) both interleavings are constructed deliberately and their frequency in a deployment is not measured; (b) the call is only misleading if Ok(idx) is read as 'the submitted entry is \\\"\n   \\\"decided at idx' - the handle documentation and the crate's example state exactly that ('durably decided at log idx'), but Entry is only Clone + Debug, so the actor cannot verify identity by comparing values and the honest fix would have to key on the assignment's ballot rather than the entry's identity; (c) both cases use a \\\"\n   \\\"2-second tick period and a long per-call deadline, so a resolution tick cannot land between the promise change and the re-synchronization; the same cases at other cadences are not measured.\\\"),\n  \\\"limitations\\\":[\n   \\\"Both interleavings are constructed deliberately; nothing is claimed about how likely either is in a deployment.\\\",\n   \\\"Whether Ok(idx) must mean 'the submitted entry is decided at idx' is a contract reading: the handle documentation and the crate's example say so ('durably decided at log idx'), but Entry is only Clone + Debug so the actor cannot verify identity by comparing values.\\\",\n   \\\"Both cases use a 2-second tick period and a long per-call deadline so that no resolution tick lands between the promise change and the re-synchronization; other cadences are not measured.\\\"]\n }]\n}\njson.dump(d,open('update.json','w'),indent